doc:sch-2-sec-12cb-ss-3 a cof:Section ;
    cof:title "12CB(3) Unconscionable conduct in connection with financial services" ;
    values:kind "subsection" ;
    values:originalText "(3) For the purpose of determining whether a person has contravened subsection (1):  (a) the court must not have regard to any circumstances that were not reasonably foreseeable at the time of the alleged contravention; and  (b) the court may have regard to conduct engaged in, or circumstances existing, before the commencement of this section." ;
    cof:pageNumber "9"^^xsd:integer .

concept:competition-and-consumer-legislation-amendment-act-2011-sch-2-sec-12cb-ss-4 a cml:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel "12CB(4) Unconscionable conduct in connection with financial services"@en ;
    cml:realizedBy doc:sch-2-sec-12cb-ss-4 ;
    values:originalText "(4) It is the intention of the Parliament that:  (a) this section is not limited by the unwritten law of the States and Territories relating to unconscionable conduct; and  (b) this section is capable of applying to a system of conduct or pattern of behaviour, whether or not a particular individual is ComLaw Authoritative Act C2011A00184  Schedule 2  Unconscionable conduct  identified as having been disadvantaged by the conduct or behaviour; and  (c) in considering whether conduct to which a contract relates is unconscionable, a court’s consideration of the contract may include consideration of:  (i) the terms of the contract; and  (ii) the manner in which and the extent to which the contract is carried out;  and is not limited to consideration of the circumstances relating to formation of the contract." ;
    skos:definition "(4) It is the intention of the Parliament that:  (a) this section is not limited by the unwritten law of the States and Territories relating to unconscionable conduct; and  (b) this section is capable of applying to a system of conduct or pattern of behaviour, whether or not a particular individual is ComLaw Authoritative Act C2011A00184  Schedule 2  Unconscionable conduct  identified as having been disadvantaged by the conduct or behaviour; and  (c) in considering whether conduct to which a contract relates is unconscionable, a court’s consideration of the contract may include consideration of:  (i) the terms of the contract; and  (ii) the manner in which and the extent to which the contract is carried out;  and is not limited to consideration of the circumstances relating to formation of the contract."@en ;
    cml:integrityHash "bf720450b830a499fedcd6f403936b40cffcc3c2a72eaa694ca157fe9a15bb01" ;
    values:partOf concept:competition-and-consumer-legislation-amendment-act-2011-sch-2-sec-12cb ;
    cof:pageNumber "9"^^xsd:integer ;
    skos:note "This section is not limited by the unwritten law of the States and Territories relating to unconscionable conduct." ;
    cml:curationStatus cml:Proposed ;
